# 26. Acceptance Criteria

CP-AUTO-005 is complete when:

- [ ] The validator discovers implemented CyberPivot MDX content.
- [ ] Foundation and OT content are both supported.
- [ ] `.gitkeep` and unrelated files are not treated as modules.
- [ ] File integrity checks are implemented for the selected CP-FILE rules.
- [ ] Frontmatter is parsed safely.
- [ ] Confirmed CP-FM rules are implemented.
- [ ] Module family rules are implemented where deterministic.
- [ ] Applicable internal consistency rules are implemented.
- [ ] The validator uses existing repository metadata/types where appropriate.
- [ ] Findings conform to CP-AUTO-004.
- [ ] Exact file locations are reported when available.
- [ ] Cascading false positives are minimized.
- [ ] No AI is used.
- [ ] No content is automatically modified.
- [ ] Exit code `1` is returned for blocking failures.
- [ ] Exit code `2` is reserved for QA execution/configuration errors.
- [ ] Existing six Foundation and two OT modules are tested.
- [ ] Representative invalid fixtures/cases are tested.
- [ ] The validator can be run locally.
- [ ] The implementation does not introduce a competing content schema.

---

# 27. Definition of Done

The first implementation milestone is successful when a developer can run the QA command against the existing CyberPivot repository and receive a trustworthy report that answers:

> "Which deterministic content rules did I violate, where did I violate them, and what do I need to inspect or fix?"

That is the only goal of CP-AUTO-005.

The semantic AI reviewer comes later.
